    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is Tekton as good as Snap-on?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "In raw material quality and core function, Tekton delivers roughly 85 to 90 percent of Snap-on's performance at around a quarter to a third of the price. Snap-on keeps genuine advantages in jaw geometry on damaged fasteners, ratchet mechanism refinement, finish quality and the tool truck service model. For most users those advantages do not justify a three-to-four-times premium; for a full-time mechanic they sometimes do.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why is Snap-on so expensive?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Three reasons. Manufacturing to tighter tolerances with proprietary geometries and largely US production costs more. The franchised tool truck model — weekly on-site visits, immediate warranty swaps, and financing — adds substantial cost that is built into the tool price. And the brand commands a premium among professionals who depend on that service and on resale value.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do professional mechanics use Tekton?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Plenty do, usually for tools they need occasionally rather than the ones they hold all day. A common pattern in professional boxes is premium ratchets and wrenches for daily drivers, with Tekton filling out socket sets, hex keys, breaker bars and specialty sizes where the price difference is hardest to justify.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is Snap-on's Flank Drive and does it matter?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It is an off-angle jaw and socket geometry that contacts the flats of a fastener rather than its corners, which reduces the tendency to round off damaged or corroded hardware. On fasteners in good condition it makes little practical difference. On rusted, partially rounded bolts it is a real advantage, which is why it matters more to a professional working on older vehicles than to a home mechanic.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How do the warranties compare?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Both cover hand tools for life. Snap-on's is delivered by the franchisee who visits your shop weekly — hand over the broken tool, get a replacement, no shipping and no downtime. Tekton's is direct with the company, requires no receipt, and replacements typically ship within a day or two. Snap-on's is faster if you are on the truck's route; Tekton's is simpler if you are not.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does Snap-on hold its value better?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, substantially. Used Snap-on hand tools retain a strong secondary market and sell readily, which offsets part of the original premium if you ever sell up. Mid-tier brands including Tekton have far weaker resale. For a professional who may change trades or retire, that residual value is a real, if rarely counted, part of the calculation.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Should a home mechanic buy Snap-on?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Generally no. The advantages Snap-on charges for — truck-side warranty swaps with zero downtime, geometry that saves seized fasteners daily, ratchet refinement felt over thousands of cycles — are professional advantages. For weekend work, the same money spread across Tekton hand tools, a good jack and quality specialist pliers buys far more capability.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Where are Tekton and Snap-on tools made?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Snap-on manufactures a large share of its hand tools in the United States, with some production elsewhere. Tekton engineers and inspects from Grand Rapids, Michigan, and manufactures primarily in Taiwan and China. Taiwanese hand tool production has been world-class for decades, so the origin difference matters less than the specification each brand demands.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
  ],
};
