    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-16 lg:py-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic border-l-4 border-[#38BDF8] pl-6 bg-gray-50 py-4 rounded-r-xl">
          Snap-on is a better tool. It is also, for most people reading this, the wrong purchase — and the reason is that a large part of what you are paying for is a service model rather than the steel in your hand.
        </p>

        <h2 id="the-price-gap" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          1. The Price Gap, Honestly Stated
        </h2>

        <p>
          Comparable hand tools from these two brands typically differ by a factor of three to four. A full combination wrench set, a socket set, a good ratchet — in every case, the Snap-on version costs several times the Tekton one. Across a complete box the difference runs to thousands of dollars, which is why this comparison generates the arguments it does.
        </p>

        <p>
          Our position after using both is straightforward: in raw material quality and core function, Tekton delivers something like <strong>85 to 90 percent</strong> of Snap-on's performance for roughly a <strong>quarter to a third</strong> of the price. That last ten to fifteen percent is real. It is concentrated in specific places — jaw geometry on damaged fasteners, ratchet refinement over thousands of cycles, finish durability, and the service model — and whether it is worth the premium depends almost entirely on how many hours a week you spend holding the tool and what happens to your income when one breaks.
        </p>

        <p>
          It is worth being clear about what the premium is not. It is not the difference between a tool that fails and one that does not: Tekton chrome vanadium wrenches take cheater bars and hammer blows without cracking, and Tekton impact sockets are correctly specified chrome molybdenum. Both brands make tools that survive normal professional use. Our <a href="/tekton-tools-review/">Tekton tools review</a> goes through that testing in detail.
        </p>

        <!-- Head-to-Head Comparison Table -->
