            </table>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="what-snap-on-does-better" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          3. What Snap-on Genuinely Does Better
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Jaw and Socket Geometry on Damaged Hardware</h3>
        <p>
          This is the most defensible part of the premium. Snap-on's off-angle drive geometry contacts the flats of a fastener rather than its corners, which distributes load away from the vulnerable edges. On new, undamaged hardware you will not notice. On a rusted, partially rounded bolt on a twenty-year-old vehicle, it is the difference between the fastener coming loose and an extraction job. A professional meets that situation weekly; a home mechanic meets it a few times a year.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Ratchet Mechanism Refinement</h3>
        <p>
          Snap-on ratchets are the benchmark for a reason. The mechanisms are smoother, the engagement is more positive, the back-drag is lower, and the feel through thousands of cycles is noticeably better than anything at a fraction of the price. Whether smoothness is worth money depends on how many hours you hold one. Over a working day it genuinely reduces fatigue; over a Saturday afternoon it is a pleasure rather than a saving.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Finish and Long-Term Wear</h3>
        <p>
          Snap-on's chrome and its polishing hold up better over years of exposure to solvents, brake cleaner and general shop abuse. Tekton's finish is genuinely good and far above budget-brand chrome, but after several years of hard professional use the difference in surface condition is visible.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Handle Ergonomics Over a Long Day</h3>
        <p>
          Ergonomics is the advantage that is hardest to appreciate in a shop aisle and most obvious after eight hours. Snap-on handle profiles, knurling depth and the balance point of its ratchets are the product of decades of iteration with people who use them all day, and the difference shows up as fewer hot spots on your palm and less grip fatigue by mid-afternoon. Tekton handles are comfortable and perfectly well shaped; they simply have not been optimised against that same feedback loop. Over a weekend project the gap is invisible. Over a working week it is the kind of thing technicians quietly cite when asked why they keep buying from the truck.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Specialty and Diagnostic Coverage</h3>
        <p>
          Snap-on's catalogue extends far beyond hand tools into diagnostics, specialist automotive tooling, and manufacturer-specific service tools that no mid-tier brand attempts. If your work requires those, the question of which brand of wrench to buy is somewhat beside the point.
        </p>

        <h2 id="where-tekton-closes-the-gap" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          4. Where Tekton Closes the Gap
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Dimensional Accuracy</h3>
        <p>
          Loose wrenches round fasteners, and this is the specification that matters most for everyday work. Tekton holds tolerance well across a set — open-end jaw widths and box-end openings measure where they should — and that consistency is what keeps hardware intact on tools in good condition. It is the single biggest reason the brand punches above its price.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Correct Material Specification</h3>
        <p>
          Tekton uses chrome vanadium for hand tools and chrome molybdenum with a black phosphate finish for impact sockets, and it labels the distinction clearly. Getting this wrong is a safety issue, and budget brands routinely blur it. Meeting the same specification as the premium brands on the material that matters removes most of the theoretical gap.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Torque Wrench Accuracy</h3>
        <p>
          Tekton click-type torque wrenches hold industry-standard tolerance through the useful part of their range, which is the same claim the premium brands make and the same claim that matters. There is no meaningful accuracy argument for spending several times more, though the premium units feel better and often have finer adjustment. Our <a href="/best-torque-wrench/">torque wrench guide</a> explains how to read accuracy claims and why the bottom of the range is where every click wrench struggles.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Sets, Organisation and Sheer Coverage</h3>
        <p>
          For the price of a modest Snap-on set you can own a very complete Tekton collection — full metric and SAE, shallow and deep, impact and chrome, hex and Torx — organised in rails and cases. Having the right size to hand beats having a slightly better tool in the wrong size, and for anyone building out a box that trade is decisive.
        </p>

        <h2 id="the-truck" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          5. The Tool Truck Model: What You Are Really Paying For
        </h2>

        <p>
          A large slice of the Snap-on premium is not in the tool at all. It is in the franchised truck that pulls into the shop every week, and understanding that is the key to the whole comparison.
        </p>

        <p>
          For a professional, the truck is a service contract disguised as a distribution channel. Break a ratchet at ten in the morning and it is replaced from the van the same week, with no shipping, no claim form, and no gap in your ability to work. The franchisee also carries stock, offers financing, and knows what the shop needs before it asks. For someone whose income stops when a tool stops, that is worth real money.
        </p>

        <p>
          For everyone else, it is a cost with no matching benefit. A home mechanic pays the same premium and receives none of the service, because there is no truck coming to a residential driveway. This is the crux of the argument: Tekton's direct-to-consumer model removes exactly the layer that professionals value and hobbyists cannot use.
        </p>

        <p>
          There is one more professional-only factor: <strong>resale</strong>. Used Snap-on hand tools hold value strongly and sell readily, so part of the premium comes back if you ever sell up or change trades. Mid-tier brands including Tekton have far weaker resale. It rarely appears in these comparisons and it belongs in the calculation for anyone treating tools as business assets.
        </p>

        <h2 id="who-should-buy-what" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          6. Who Should Buy Which
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Buy Tekton If</h3>
        <p>
          You are a home mechanic, a serious DIYer, a maintenance technician who is not on a truck route, or a professional filling gaps in an existing box. You want broad coverage now rather than a premium set assembled over years. You have no use for a weekly van visit, and you would rather spend the difference on a better jack, a decent impact wrench, or the specialist pliers and drivers that neither brand leads on.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Buy Snap-on If</h3>
        <p>
          You turn wrenches full time, especially on older or corroded vehicles where jaw geometry earns its keep every day. Downtime costs you money, so a truck-side swap beats any mail-in process. You hold a ratchet for hours and the refinement translates into less fatigue. And you value the resale floor under a large investment in tools.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">The Realistic Answer: Both</h3>
        <p>
          Most experienced boxes are mixed, and sensibly so. Premium ratchets and the wrench sizes you reach for every day; mid-tier for the rest — the sockets you use twice a year, hex keys, breaker bars, the third set of pliers. Spending premium money on the tools you hold constantly and mid-tier money on the ones that sit in the drawer is simply better allocation than brand loyalty in either direction. Our <a href="/tekton-vs-gearwrench/">Tekton vs GearWrench comparison</a> covers the other side of that mid-tier decision.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Common Mistakes in This Decision</h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Premium Tools for Occasional Use</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The advantages Snap-on charges for accrue with hours of use and with the frequency of difficult fasteners. Spread across a few weekends a year, they never accumulate into value — the money buys more capability elsewhere.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Price Difference Equals Quality Difference</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A three-to-four-times price gap does not mean a three-to-four-times quality gap. Much of the premium funds a service model. The steel specification is the same category on both sides; the refinement differs at the margins.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Warranty You Can Actually Use</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Snap-on's warranty is superb if a truck visits your shop and largely theoretical if it does not. Tekton's mails a replacement to any address without a receipt. Choose the one that matches how you will actually claim.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Financing a Tool Box You Do Not Yet Need</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Truck financing has put many young technicians into payments that outlast their enthusiasm for the trade. Build capability first with mid-tier tools, then upgrade the specific ones that prove themselves worth it in daily use.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Overlooking Where Neither Brand Leads</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                For pliers and screwdrivers, the European specialists — Knipex, Wera, Wiha — beat both on the tools you use most. Allocating some of the budget there produces a better box than spending it all with one badge.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Verdict -->
        <div id="verdict" class="my-16 bg-gradient-to-br from-gray-950 to-gray-900 rounded-3xl p-8 lg:p-12 text-white shadow-2xl relative overflow-hidden">
          <div class="absolute top-0 right-0 bg-[#FF4500] text-white font-black text-xs uppercase tracking-widest px-4 py-2 rounded-bl-xl">
            ★ Our Verdict
          </div>
          <h2 id="who-wins" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-white mt-0 mb-6 border-none">Tekton vs Snap-on: Who Wins?</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6 text-gray-300">
            <p>
              <strong class="text-[#38BDF8]">Choose Tekton if:</strong> tools are not how you earn your living every day. You get roughly 85 to 90 percent of the performance for a quarter to a third of the price, correct material specifications, tolerances that keep fasteners intact, and a warranty you can claim from anywhere without a receipt.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong class="text-[#FF4500]">Choose Snap-on if:</strong> you turn wrenches professionally, work on corroded hardware regularly, and cannot afford downtime. The jaw geometry, the ratchet refinement and the truck-side warranty are worth real money in that context — and the strong resale market puts a floor under the investment.
            </p>
            <p class="text-lg border-t border-gray-700 pt-6 mt-6">
              <strong class="text-white">The Honest Bottom Line:</strong> Snap-on is the better tool and Tekton is the better buy for most of the people asking. The premium is real but it is concentrated in professional advantages — damaged-fastener geometry, cycle-life refinement, and a service model that keeps a shop working. If none of those describe your week, buy Tekton, buy the sizes you actually need, and spend the difference on something that expands what you can do rather than on a marginally nicer version of what you already have.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Tekton as good as Snap-on?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">In material quality and core function, Tekton delivers roughly 85 to 90 percent of Snap-on's performance at a quarter to a third of the price. Snap-on retains real advantages in jaw geometry on damaged fasteners, ratchet refinement, finish durability and the truck service model — advantages that matter far more to a full-time mechanic than to a weekend one.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is Snap-on so expensive?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Tighter tolerances, proprietary geometries and largely US manufacturing cost more; the franchised tool truck — weekly visits, instant warranty swaps, financing — adds substantial cost built into the price; and the brand commands a premium among professionals who rely on that service and on resale value.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Do professional mechanics use Tekton?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Many do, typically for tools they need occasionally rather than hold all day. The common pattern is premium ratchets and daily-driver wrenches, with Tekton filling out socket sets, hex keys, breaker bars and specialty sizes where the price gap is hardest to justify.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What is Flank Drive and does it matter?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">It is Snap-on's off-angle geometry, which contacts a fastener's flats rather than its corners and reduces rounding on damaged hardware. On sound fasteners it makes little difference; on rusted, partially rounded bolts it is a genuine advantage — which is why it matters more on old vehicles than on new ones.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How do the warranties compare?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Both are lifetime on hand tools. Snap-on's is delivered by the franchisee visiting your shop — hand over the broken tool, get a replacement, no downtime. Tekton's is direct with the company, needs no receipt, and usually ships within a day or two. Snap-on's is faster on a truck route; Tekton's is simpler everywhere else.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Snap-on hold its value better?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Substantially. Used Snap-on hand tools have a strong secondary market and sell readily, recovering part of the original premium. Mid-tier brands including Tekton have much weaker resale, which is worth counting if you treat tools as business assets.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Should a home mechanic buy Snap-on?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Usually not. The advantages being charged for are professional ones — zero-downtime warranty swaps, geometry that saves seized fasteners daily, refinement felt over thousands of cycles. For weekend work the same money spread across Tekton tools, a good jack and quality pliers buys far more capability.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Where are these tools made?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Snap-on manufactures a large share of its hand tools in the United States. Tekton engineers and inspects from Grand Rapids, Michigan, and manufactures primarily in Taiwan and China — where hand tool production has been world-class for decades. The specification each brand demands matters more than the address.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

