          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Snap-on</strong> earns its reputation on jaw geometry that saves damaged fasteners, ratchets that stay smooth over years of daily use, and a service model that keeps a working shop working. <strong class="text-white">Tekton</strong> delivers the same class of steel, correct material specifications and tolerances that hold, without the truck, the franchise or the markup.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
            If you turn wrenches for a living on old vehicles, the premium is defensible and the resale market softens it further. If you do not, buy Tekton, buy broadly, and put the difference towards tools that extend what you can do. The best box is nearly always mixed — premium where your hands live, mid-tier everywhere else.
